----------
exposure : number, stops, -10 to 10, default 0.0
    Brightness in photographic stops: +1 doubles every value, -1 halves it.
contrast : number, ratio, 0 to 5, default 1.0
    How far values are pushed away from the pivot: 1 = unchanged, 2 = twice as far, 0 =
    flat at the pivot.
pivot : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    The brightness (0..1) that contrast leaves where it is; values above it brighten,
    below it darken.
saturation : number, ratio, 0 to 5, default 1.0
    Colourfulness: 1 = unchanged, 0 = grey (Rec. 709 brightness kept), above 1 more
    vivid."""
    ...

def gradient_map(stops: collections.abc.Sequence[str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float]] = ('#000000', '#ffffff'), shadow_clip: float = 0.01, highlight_clip: float = 0.01, quantile_method: Literal['linear', 'lower', 'higher', 'nearest', 'midpoint', 'inverted_cdf', 'averaged_inverted_cdf', 'closest_observation', 'interpolated_inverted_cdf', 'hazen', 'weibull', 'median_unbiased', 'normal_unbiased'] = 'linear') -> Step:
    """Recolour by brightness: darkest pixels get the first stop, brightest the last, evenly spaced between.

Two stops make a duotone, three a tritone; e.g. stops = ["#0b0020", "#ff2bd6", "#00f0ff"] is a neon scheme.
The picture is first stretched to use its full tonal range (cutting `shadow_clip` and `highlight_clip` off its
ends), so the whole gradient is always visible.

Parameters
----------
stops : list of colours, default ('#000000', '#ffffff')
    The gradient's colours from darkest to brightest, evenly spaced; at least two. Two
    make a duotone, three a tritone.
shadow_clip : number, fraction, 0 to 0.5, default 0.01
    Share of the darkest pixels (0..0.5) that the contrast stretch pushes onto the first
    stop; 0 maps only the darkest pixel there. Larger gives deeper shadows.
highlight_clip : number, fraction, 0 to 0.5, default 0.01
    Share of the brightest pixels (0..0.5) that the contrast stretch pushes onto the
    last stop. Larger gives brighter highlights.
quantile_method : one of 'linear', 'lower', 'higher', 'nearest', 'midpoint', 'inverted_cdf', 'averaged_inverted_cdf', 'closest_observation', 'interpolated_inverted_cdf', 'hazen', 'weibull', 'median_unbiased', 'normal_unbiased', default 'linear'
    How the stretch's clip points are read from the picture's brightness (numpy's
    quantile methods): 'linear' interpolates between the two nearest pixels; 'lower',
    'higher', 'nearest' and 'midpoint' take the darker, the brighter, the nearer pixel
    or their mean; 'inverted_cdf', 'averaged_inverted_cdf', 'closest_observation',
    'interpolated_inverted_cdf', 'hazen', 'weibull', 'median_unbiased' and
    'normal_unbiased' are the textbook sample-quantile definitions (Hyndman and Fan).
    Only visible on small or flat pictures."""
    ...

def grain(amount: float = 0.08, size: float = 1.0, seed: int = 0, truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Film grain: brightness noise of standard deviation `amount` (0.08 visible, 0.2 heavy), clumped to `size` px.

The same noise is added to every channel, so the grain is monochrome; the result is clipped to 0..1. The same
`seed` always gives the same grain, so renders are repeatable.

Parameters
----------
amount : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.08
    Standard deviation of the added brightness, in 0..1 picture units: 0 = none, 0.08 is
    visible, 0.2 is heavy.
size : number, px, 0 to 50, default 1.0
    Grain size in px: up to 1 every pixel varies on its own; above 1 the noise is
    blurred (Gaussian sigma = size / 2) into specks about this wide, keeping its
    standard deviation. Larger = coarser, softer grain.
seed : whole number, count, 0 to 4294967295, default 0
    Random seed: the same seed always gives the same grain, another seed a different
    pattern. Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to the nearest whole number).
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the clumping blur's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas (only when size > 1).
    Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the clumping blur sees beyond the picture's edge (only when size > 1):
    'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel),
    'nearest' (the edge pixel repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge, so the grain tiles
    seamlessly)."""
    ...

def halation(radius: float = 20.0, threshold: float = 0.7, amount: float = 0.7, color: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#ff4a14', truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """A coloured glow around the highlights: where the brightest channel is above `threshold` it is blurred by
`radius` px, tinted `color` and screened over the picture by `amount`.

Real halation comes from light that passes through the film base and bounces back, exposing the red-sensitive
layer around bright spots, so the glow takes the tint whatever the colour of the highlight itself. It only ever
brightens and leaves black black.

Parameters
----------
radius : number, px, 0 to 500, default 20.0
    Gaussian sigma of the glow in px at the recipe's size: larger spreads the glow
    further from the highlights; 0 keeps it on the highlights themselves.
threshold : number, fraction, 0 to 0.99, default 0.7
    The brightness (0..1) where the glow starts, judged on the brightest of a pixel's
    three channels: only values above it glow, rising to full at 1. Raising it limits
    the halation to the brightest highlights.
amount : number, ratio, 0 to 10, default 0.7
    How strongly the tinted glow is screened over the picture: 0 = off, 1 = the glow at
    full brightness; above 1 it saturates sooner.
color : colour, default '#ff4a14'
    The colour of the glow: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1. The default is the red-
    orange of film's red-sensitive layer; white makes a neutral glow.
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the glow's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas: it reaches truncate x radius px.
    Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the glow's blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated) or 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def halftone(cell: int = 14, angle: float = 45.0, ink: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] | None = None, ink_floor: float = 0.35, paper: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#000000', paper_threshold: float = 0.5, sigma: float = 0.5, feather: float = 1.0, truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'constant', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect', constant_value: float = 0.0) -> Step:
    """Print-style dot screen: tone becomes dot size. `cell` = distance between dots in px, `angle` = screen rotation.

A dot's area is proportional to the ink its tone needs, so the screen keeps the picture's tones; dots touch at
half tone and fill their cell at full tone. `ink` = one colour for every dot; leave it out to take each dot's
colour from the photo.

Parameters
----------
cell : whole number, px, 1 to 200, default 14
    Distance between neighbouring dots in px (the screen's pitch). Larger = fewer,
    bigger dots, a coarser print. Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to the
    nearest whole number).
angle : number, degrees, -360 to 360, default 45.0
    Rotation of the dot grid in degrees, clockwise on screen: rows of dots run that many
    degrees below the x axis (negative = above). The grid is square, so 90 degrees on
    looks the same; 45 is the classic screen angle for one ink.
ink : colour or none, default None
    The ink: one colour for every dot ('#rrggbb', '#rgb' or three numbers 0..1); leave
    it out to give each dot the colour of the photo under it, brightened so small dots
    stay visible (by at most 1 / `ink_floor`).
ink_floor : number, fraction, 0.01 to 1, default 0.35
    With no `ink`: the most a dot's colour is brightened, as a floor 0.01 to 1 on its
    strongest channel: a colour whose strongest channel is below it is scaled by 1 /
    ink_floor instead of up to full, so dark areas stay dark. Lowering it brightens dark
    colours more; 1 = colours as they are.
paper : colour, default '#000000'
    The paper colour between the dots. On a dark paper (Rec. 709 luminance below
    `paper_threshold`) bright tones get the ink; on a light one, dark tones.
paper_threshold : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    The Rec. 709 luminance, 0 to 1, below which `paper` counts as dark (bright tones get
    the ink) rather than light (dark tones get it); 0.5 = mid grey. Raising it lets
    lighter paper count as dark: 0 treats every paper as light, 1 every paper but white
    as dark.
sigma : number, ratio, 0 to 5, default 0.5
    Gaussian sigma, in cells, of the blur that averages the tone each dot stands for: 0
    = each dot sees only the pixel at its centre (detail, but noisy), larger = smoother
    tone that spreads into neighbouring dots.
feather : number, px, 0.1 to 50, default 1.0
    Width of each dot's soft edge in px (anti-aliasing): small = crisp dots, larger =
    blurrier dots. The ink stays the same.
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the tone blur's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas: it reaches truncate x sigma
    cells. Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'constant', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the tone blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge), 'constant' (constant_value).
constant_value : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.0
    The brightness (0..1) assumed beyond the edge when mode is 'constant'; ignored
    otherwise."""
    ...

def highpass(radius: float = 4.0, truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Keep only the detail finer than `radius` px: the picture minus its Gaussian blur, centred on mid-grey.

A flat area becomes mid-grey, an edge a dark line on its dark side and a bright one on its bright side. Each
colour channel is filtered on its own and the result is kept within 0..1.

Parameters
----------
radius : number, px, 0 to 100, default 4.0
    Gaussian sigma of the blurred copy in px at the recipe's size: detail finer than
    this is kept, coarser tones are removed. Small radii keep only the finest texture,
    large ones keep edges and local contrast; 0 keeps nothing but mid-grey.
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the blur's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas: the blur reaches truncate x radius
    px. Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def hue_shift(hue: float = 90.0) -> Step:
    """Rotate every hue around the colour wheel by `hue` degrees: the quickest way to try another colour scheme.

Brightness is preserved (as `luminance` measures it), so the picture keeps its lighting while colours change,
except for very saturated colours whose rotated version cannot be displayed and gets clipped.

Parameters
----------
hue : number, degrees, -360 to 360, default 90.0
    The turn around the colour wheel: 120 sends red to green, 360 is a full turn
    (unchanged)."""
    ...

def invert() -> Step:
    """Turn the picture into its negative: every red, green and blue value v becomes 1 - v.

Black becomes white, white black, mid grey stays mid grey and each colour becomes its complement, so a warm
picture turns cool. Doing it twice gives back the original picture.

Parameters
----------
"""
    ...

def lens_blur(radius: float = 8.0, highlights: float = 0.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Blur the picture over a disc of `radius` px, as an out-of-focus lens does, favouring bright pixels.

Every pixel becomes the average of the pixels within `radius` px of it (one on the rim counts for the share of it
inside the disc, a linear ramp one pixel wide), each weighted by 1 + `highlights` x its brightness. Flat areas
stay flat and the result never leaves the picture's own range. The cost hardly grows with the radius.

Parameters
----------
radius : number, px, 0 to 100, default 8.0
    The radius of the disc in px at the recipe's size: larger is further out of focus
    and spreads each point into a bigger circle; 0 leaves the picture unchanged.
highlights : number, ratio, 0 to 50, default 0.0
    How much brighter pixels count in the average: a pixel of brightness b (0..1) counts
    1 + highlights x b times as much as a black one, so with 0 every pixel counts the
    same (a plain average), and raising it turns bright lights into distinct, luminous
    discs (bokeh) while dark areas hardly bleed into them.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def levels(black: float = 0.0, white: float = 1.0, gamma: float = 1.0, output_black: float = 0.0, output_white: float = 1.0) -> Step:
    """Remap tones with an input black point, white point and midtone `gamma`, then an output range.

Each channel is normalised between `black` and `white` and clipped to 0..1, raised to `1 / gamma`, and placed
between `output_black` and `output_white`. The defaults change nothing.

Parameters
----------
black : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.0
    The input black point (0..1): values at or below it become the output black. Raising
    it darkens shadows and adds contrast.
white : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 1.0
    The input white point (0..1): values at or above it become the output white.
    Lowering it brightens highlights and adds contrast. At or below `black` the picture
    is cut at `black` into the two output ends.
gamma : number, ratio, 0.1 to 10, default 1.0
    The midtone gamma: 1 = linear, above 1 brightens the midtones, below 1 darkens them;
    black and white stay where they are (output = input ** (1 / gamma), as in
    Photoshop).
output_black : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.0
    The darkest value that can be output (0..1): raising it lifts the blacks to a matte
    grey. Above `output_white` the tones are reversed.
output_white : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 1.0
    The brightest value that can be output (0..1): lowering it dims the whites."""
    ...

def motion_blur(angle: float = 0.0, length: float = 24.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Smear the picture along a straight line of `length` px at `angle` degrees, as a moving camera would.

Every pixel becomes the average of the picture along that line, each colour channel on its own, so flat areas stay
flat, the mean is kept and the result never leaves the picture's own range. The cost hardly grows with the length.

Parameters
----------
angle : number, degrees, 0 to 360, default 0.0
    The direction of the motion, counter-clockwise as seen in the picture: 0 =
    horizontal, 90 = vertical, 45 = rising to the right. Opposite directions (0 and 180)
    give the same blur.
length : number, px, 0 to 500, default 24.0
    How far the smear reaches along the line, end to end, in px at the recipe's size:
    larger is a faster motion; 0 leaves the picture unchanged.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def palette_quantize(palette: Literal['photo', 'gameboy', 'cga', 'ega', 'pico8'] = 'photo', colors: int = 8, init: Literal['k-means++', 'random'] = 'k-means++', iterations: int = 20, tolerance: float = 0.001, sample: int = 10000, seed: int = 0) -> Step:
    """Reduce the picture to a few colours: `colors` found in it by k-means, or a named `palette`.

Distances are measured in Oklab, so a pixel gets the colour that looks closest to it, not the one with the nearest
red, green and blue numbers. With palette 'photo' the colours are searched on `sample` random pixels, then every
pixel is given its nearest colour and each colour becomes the average of the pixels it replaced, so the picture's
overall colour is kept. With a named palette the pixels are painted in the palette's exact colours.

Parameters
----------
palette : one of 'photo', 'gameboy', 'cga', 'ega', 'pico8', default 'photo'
    Where the colours come from. 'photo' finds `colors` colours in the picture itself
    (k-means); the others are fixed palettes: 'gameboy' (the 4 greens widely used to
    draw a Game Boy screen), 'cga' (black, cyan, magenta, white), 'ega' (the 16 IBM EGA
    colours) and 'pico8' (the 16 PICO-8 colours). Every pixel gets the nearest colour in
    Oklab, a space where equal distances look equally different.
colors : whole number, count, 2 to 256, default 8
    How many colours to find when `palette` is 'photo' (ignored for a fixed palette).
    Raising it keeps more detail and gradation; fewer than this many are used if the
    picture has fewer distinct colours. Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to
    the nearest whole number).
init : one of 'k-means++', 'random', default 'k-means++'
    How the search for colours starts (palette 'photo'): 'k-means++' spreads the
    starting colours out (Arthur and Vassilvitskii, 2007) and usually finds a better
    set; 'random' starts from `colors` random pixels.
iterations : whole number, count, 1 to 100, default 20
    The most k-means rounds to run when `palette` is 'photo'. Each round moves every
    colour to the average of the pixels nearest to it; raising it lets the search settle
    further. Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to the nearest whole number).
tolerance : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.001
    Stop the k-means rounds early once no colour has moved farther than this in Oklab
    (where black to white is 1). 0 runs all `iterations`; raising it stops sooner with a
    rougher result.
sample : whole number, count, 1 to 1000000, default 10000
    How many randomly chosen pixels the colours are searched on when `palette` is
    'photo'; every pixel is still repainted. A picture with fewer pixels is searched
    whole. Raising it costs time and can find rare colours; lowering it is faster but
    may miss small areas of colour. Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to the
    nearest whole number).
seed : whole number, count, 0 to 4294967295, default 0
    Random seed for the choice of sample pixels and starting colours: the same seed
    always gives the same picture, another seed may find slightly different colours.
    Animated, it moves in whole steps (rounded to the nearest whole number)."""
    ...

def posterize(levels: int = 256) -> Step:
    """Reduce every colour channel to `levels` evenly spaced tones, each value snapping to the nearest one.

The levels run from 0 to 1 in steps of 1 / (levels - 1), so black and white are always kept. Doing it twice
changes nothing more.

Parameters
----------
levels : whole number, count, 2 to 256, default 256
    Number of evenly spaced tone levels kept in each of red, green and blue, black and
    white included: 2 gives pure black or full intensity per channel (8 colours), 4
    gives 0, 1/3, 2/3 and 1. Lowering it flattens the tones into fewer, coarser bands;
    256 only rounds to the 8-bit levels of a JPEG or PNG. Animated, it moves in whole
    steps (rounded to the nearest whole number)."""
    ...

def radial_blur(mode: Literal['spin', 'zoom'] = 'zoom', amount: float = 0.1, x: float = 0.5, y: float = 0.5, samples: int = 8, interpolation: Literal['nearest', 'bilinear', 'bicubic'] = 'bilinear', edge: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Smear the picture around a centre (`mode` 'spin') or away from it ('zoom'), by `amount`.

Every pixel becomes the average of `samples` points spread evenly along the arc or ray through it (centred on the
pixel), each read by `interpolation`, each colour channel on its own. The centre stays sharp, flat areas
stay flat and the result never leaves the picture's own range.

Parameters
----------
mode : one of 'spin', 'zoom', default 'zoom'
    The direction of the smear: 'spin' averages along the circle around the centre, as a
    rotating camera or wheel would blur; 'zoom' averages along the ray through the
    centre, as zooming the lens during the exposure would. The centre itself stays sharp
    in both.
amount : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.1
    How far each pixel is smeared. 'spin': the arc, as a fraction of a full turn (0.1 =
    36 degrees, 1 = a whole circle), centred on the pixel. 'zoom': the range of scales,
    as a fraction of the pixel's distance from the centre (0.1 = from 5% closer to 5%
    farther, 1 = from half to one and a half times the distance). Larger is a longer
    smear and stronger at the edges; 0 leaves the picture unchanged.
x : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    The centre's horizontal position as a fraction of the way across the picture: 0 =
    the first pixel column, 0.5 = the middle, 1 = the last.
y : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    The centre's vertical position as a fraction of the way down the picture: 0 = the
    first pixel row, 0.5 = the middle, 1 = the last.
samples : whole number, count, 1 to 256, default 8
    How many points along each arc or ray are averaged, spread evenly over its length
    and read from the picture by the chosen interpolation: more is smoother and slower
    (the time grows with it), and a smear of more than a few pixels per sample shows as
    separate ghost copies. With 1 the picture is unchanged. Animated, it moves in whole
    steps (rounded to the nearest whole number).
interpolation : one of 'nearest', 'bilinear', 'bicubic', default 'bilinear'
    How a point between pixels is read from the picture: 'nearest' takes the closest
    pixel (fastest, keeps every value exactly as it is but steps and ghosts show in
    smooth areas), 'bilinear' blends the four pixels around it (smooth, slightly soft),
    'bicubic' fits a cubic spline through the neighbouring pixels (sharpest and most
    exact on smooth gradients, a little slower, and may ring beside hard edges).
edge : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the smear sees beyond the picture's edge (arcs and rays near the corners leave
    it): 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge
    pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def selective_color(hue: float = 0.0, width: float = 60.0, feather: float = 30.0, hue_shift: float = 0.0, saturation: float = 1.0, lightness: float = 0.0) -> Step:
    """Shift the hue, saturation and lightness of the colours in one hue range, leaving every other pixel untouched.

Works in HSV on the gamma-encoded values. A pixel's weight is its place in the range (1 within `width` of the
`hue` centre, falling linearly to 0 over `feather` degrees; the wheel wraps round) times its HSV saturation, so a
grey, which has no hue, is never selected and a pale colour is changed less than a vivid one. Pixels of weight 0
come back bit for bit. A selected pixel moves by weight times each change: `hue_shift` degrees, saturation times
`saturation` and value toward black or full by `lightness`. Saturation and value stay inside 0..1 by
construction, so the result needs no clipping.

Parameters
----------
hue : number, degrees, 0 to 360, default 0.0
    Centre of the range on the colour wheel, 0..360 degrees: 0 red, 60 yellow, 120
    green, 180 cyan, 240 blue, 300 magenta; 360 is red again.
width : number, degrees, 0 to 360, default 60.0
    Full width of the range, 0..360 degrees, centred on `hue`; colours inside it are
    changed at full strength. 360 takes in every colour; raising it widens the range.
feather : number, degrees, 0 to 180, default 30.0
    How far beyond each edge of the range the change fades out, 0..180 degrees: the
    weight falls in a straight line from full at the edge to none that far away. 0 gives
    a hard edge; raising it blends the range into its neighbours.
hue_shift : number, degrees, -180 to 180, default 0.0
    How far the selected hues are turned round the colour wheel, -180..180 degrees;
    positive goes red toward yellow, green, blue. 0 keeps the hue.
saturation : number, ratio, 0 to 3, default 1.0
    Gain on the saturation of the selected colours: 1 keeps it, 0 turns them grey, above
    1 makes them more vivid (stopping at full saturation).
lightness : number, fraction, -1 to 1, default 0.0
    Brightness (HSV value) of the selected colours, -1..1: -1 takes them all the way to
    black, +1 all the way to the full value, 0 keeps it; a value of 0.5 closes half the
    gap in that direction."""
    ...

def shadows_highlights(shadows: float = 0.5, highlights: float = 0.0, radius: float = 30.0, edge_threshold: float = 0.1) -> Step:
    """Lighten the dark areas and darken the bright ones of the picture, as judged by an edge-aware local brightness.

The base layer B (0..1) is the Rec. 709 luma of the sRGB-encoded picture smoothed by a guided filter (He, Sun and
Tang 2010, guided by the luma itself) over a window of 2 x `radius` + 1 px, which averages flat areas but keeps
its edges. Every channel c then becomes c^e with e = (1 + `highlights` B^2) / (1 + `shadows` (1 - B)^2): a gamma
curve whose exponent follows the surroundings B, not the pixel, so a dark pixel in a bright area is lifted less
than the same pixel in a dark one. Raising `shadows` only lowers e (lifting c towards 1) and raising
`highlights` only raises it (pulling c towards 0), black and white stay where they are, and 0..1 maps into 0..1.

Parameters
----------
shadows : number, ratio, 0 to 4, default 0.5
    How much dark areas are lightened: 0 = off. In the darkest surroundings a value v
    becomes v^(1 / (1 + shadows)), a gamma of 1 + shadows (1 = square root, 3 = fourth
    root); surroundings that are brighter are lifted less, and areas brighter than mid
    grey hardly at all. Black stays black.
highlights : number, ratio, 0 to 4, default 0.0
    How much bright areas are darkened: 0 = off. In the brightest surroundings a value v
    becomes v^(1 + highlights), a gamma of 1 + highlights (1 = squared, 3 = to the
    fourth power); surroundings that are darker are darkened less, and areas darker than
    mid grey hardly at all. White stays white.
radius : number, px, 0 to 500, default 30.0
    Size of the surroundings that decide whether an area is dark or bright, in px at the
    recipe's size (rounded to whole px): the base layer is an average over a square of 2
    x radius + 1 px. Larger judges bigger regions and recovers whole dark or bright
    areas; smaller follows fine detail; 0 judges every pixel by its own brightness (a
    plain tone curve).
edge_threshold : number, fraction, 0.001 to 1, default 0.1
    The brightness step (0..1, Rec. 709 luma) that counts as an edge the base layer must
    not blur across: steps above it keep their own side of the base, which avoids halos
    around them; steps below it are averaged away. Raising it makes the base smoother
    and more like a plain blur; lowering it follows ever smaller edges."""
    ...

def sharpen(radius: float = 2.0, amount: float = 0.8, threshold: float = 0.0, truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """Sharpen with an unsharp mask: add `amount` times the detail that a `radius` px Gaussian blur removes.

Detail smaller than `threshold` is left as it is; each colour channel is sharpened on its own, and the result is
kept within 0..1.

Parameters
----------
radius : number, px, 0 to 100, default 2.0
    Gaussian sigma of the blurred copy in px at the recipe's size: small radii sharpen
    fine texture, large ones add local contrast around coarse edges; 0 leaves the
    picture unchanged.
amount : number, ratio, 0 to 5, default 0.8
    How much of the detail is added back: 0 = off, 1 = the full difference from the
    blurred copy; above 1 edges overshoot and halos show.
threshold : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.0
    The smallest difference (0..1, per channel) from the blurred copy that is sharpened:
    smaller differences are left alone, which keeps grain and smooth skin from being
    sharpened; 0 sharpens everything.
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the blur's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas: the blur reaches truncate x radius
    px. Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated), 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def soft_focus(radius: float = 12.0, opacity: float = 0.5, truncate: float = 4.0, mode: Literal['reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap'] = 'reflect') -> Step:
    """A dreamy glow over the whole picture: a copy blurred by `radius` px is screened over the sharp one.

`opacity` sets how much of that replaces the sharp picture. This is the Orton effect of film photography (a sharp
and a blurred exposure of the same scene stacked). The picture only ever gets
brighter, detail stays under the glow, and black stays black.

Parameters
----------
radius : number, px, 0 to 500, default 12.0
    Gaussian sigma of the blurred copy in px at the recipe's size: larger is dreamier
    and softer; 0 blurs nothing, so the picture is only brightened by screening it with
    itself.
opacity : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    How much of the screened result replaces the sharp picture: 0 = off (the picture
    unchanged), 1 = the full soft focus. Raising it brightens and softens more.
truncate : number, ratio, 1 to 10, default 4.0
    Where the blur's Gaussian is cut off, in sigmas: it reaches truncate x radius px.
    Larger is more exact and slower.
mode : one of 'reflect', 'nearest', 'mirror', 'wrap', default 'reflect'
    What the blur sees beyond the picture's edge: 'reflect' (mirrored, edge pixel
    repeated), 'mirror' (mirrored about the edge pixel), 'nearest' (the edge pixel
    repeated) or 'wrap' (the opposite edge)."""
    ...

def solarize(threshold: float = 0.5) -> Step:
    """Invert every red, green and blue value that is above `threshold` and leave the others as they are.

The tones fold back on themselves at the threshold, the look of an over-exposed darkroom print (the Sabattier
effect): shadows stay, highlights turn dark, and a bright edge appears where the two meet.

Parameters
----------
threshold : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    Channel value (0..1) above which red, green and blue are inverted; values at or
    below it stay. Lowering it reverses more of the tones (0 inverts everything but pure
    black); 1 changes nothing."""
    ...

def split_tone(shadow: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#3a1bff', highlight: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#ff2bd6', amount: float = 0.3, balance: float = 0.5, softness: float = 0.5, black_fade: float = 0.1) -> Step:
    """Tint the dark parts with `shadow` and the bright parts with `highlight`, without changing brightness.

The subtle way to get a neon or cinematic cast: skin tones and detail stay, the colour shifts toward the tints.
`amount` 0..1 (0.2 is a hint, 0.6 is strong), `balance` = the brightness where one tint hands over to the other.
Pure black stays black (a tint fades in up to `black_fade` brightness), so a black background is not coloured.
Near pure white a tint would clip, so those few pixels shift slightly in brightness.

Parameters
----------
shadow : colour, default '#3a1bff'
    The tint of the dark parts: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1.
highlight : colour, default '#ff2bd6'
    The tint of the bright parts: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1.
amount : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.3
    How strongly the tints are added: 0 = off, 0.2 a hint, 0.6 strong.
balance : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    The brightness (0..1) where the shadow tint hands over to the highlight tint.
    Raising it tints more of the picture with the shadow colour.
softness : number, fraction, 0.01 to 1, default 0.5
    Width of the brightness band (0.01..1), centred on balance, over which one tint
    blends into the other. Smaller makes a sharper hand-over.
black_fade : number, fraction, 0.001 to 1, default 0.1
    The brightness (0.001..1) up to which the tints fade in from nothing, so pure black
    stays black. Larger keeps more of the shadows untinted."""
    ...

def threshold(level: float = 0.5, dark: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#000000', light: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#ffffff') -> Step:
    """Turn the picture into a two-colour bitmap: `light` where its brightness reaches `level`, `dark` elsewhere.

Brightness is Rec. 709 luminance, so a pure green is far brighter than a pure blue, as the eye sees them.

Parameters
----------
level : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    Brightness (Rec. 709 luminance, 0 = black, 1 = white) at which the picture is split:
    pixels at or above it are painted `light`, the rest `dark`. Raising it turns more of
    the picture dark; 0 makes all of it light.
dark : colour, default '#000000'
    Colour of the pixels darker than `level`: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1.
light : colour, default '#ffffff'
    Colour of the pixels at or above `level`: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1."""
    ...

def tone_map(contrast: float = 0.5, amount: float = 0.5, radius: float = 30.0, edge_threshold: float = 0.5) -> Step:
    """Compress the broad brightness differences of the picture and keep (or boost) its fine detail.

Brightness is the base 2 logarithm L of the Rec. 709 luma Y of the sRGB-encoded picture (Y floored at 1e-4), in
stops below white. A guided filter (He, Sun and Tang 2010, guided by L itself) over a window of 2 x `radius` + 1 px,
edge-preserving by `edge_threshold` stops, splits it into a base B and detail D = L - B (Durand and Dorsey 2002).
The new log brightness is `contrast` x B + (1 + `amount`) x D, and every pixel's red, green and blue are
multiplied by 2^(new - L) = 2^((`contrast` - 1) B + `amount` D), so hue and saturation stay and the
result is held to 0..1. An even area of luma Y comes out as Y^`contrast`. B is an average of values of L, so it
never leaves their range: with `amount` 0 lowering `contrast` only ever brightens.

Parameters
----------
contrast : number, ratio, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    How much of the broad brightness differences between areas is kept: 1 = all of them
    (no compression), 0.5 = halved in stops (a picture with dark areas three stops below
    its bright ones gets them one and a half apart), 0 = every area brought up to white
    and only the detail left. Lowering it lightens dark areas the most; white stays
    white.
amount : number, ratio, 0 to 5, default 0.5
    How strongly the fine detail (what is left of the brightness once the broad areas
    are taken out) is exaggerated: 0 = kept as it is, 1 = doubled in stops. Raising it
    brings out texture; it can clip highlights.
radius : number, px, 0 to 500, default 30.0
    Size of what counts as a broad area, in px at the recipe's size (rounded to whole
    px): the base layer is an average over a square of 2 x radius + 1 px. Larger treats
    bigger structures as base and keeps only finer ones as detail; 0 makes every pixel
    its own base, a plain global curve (a grey v becomes v^contrast).
edge_threshold : number, stops, 0.01 to 8, default 0.5
    The brightness step, in stops, that counts as an edge the base layer must not blur
    across: steps above it keep their own side of the base, which avoids halos around
    them; steps below it are averaged away and count as detail. Raising it makes the
    base smoother and more like a plain blur."""
    ...

def vibrance(amount: float = 0.0) -> Step:
    """Raise the saturation of muted colours more than that of vivid ones, never lowering anyone's.

Works on HSV saturation s = (max - min) / max of the gamma-encoded values. Each colour's saturation becomes
s + amount * s * (1 - s), which never exceeds 1, so the result stays inside 0..1 without clipping. The strongest
channel (HSV value) is kept and every other channel moves away from it by the same factor, so the hue is exactly
the input's (up to float rounding) and a grey, whose saturation is 0, is unchanged.

Parameters
----------
amount : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.0
    How far muted colours are pushed toward full saturation (0..1): 0 changes nothing, 1
    raises a colour's saturation s to s + s * (1 - s), so a colour at 0.2 goes to 0.36
    while one at 0.9 only reaches 0.99. Greys and fully saturated colours stay as they
    are; colours are never made duller."""
    ...

def vignette(amount: float = 0.5, radius: float = 0.5, feather: float = 0.5, roundness: float = 1.0, color: str | list[float] | tuple[float, float, float] = '#000000') -> Step:
    """Fade the picture toward `color` from `radius` out to its corners, the centre untouched.

A pixel's distance from the centre is 0 in the middle and 1 at a corner; the contours of equal distance are
superellipses through the corners (`roundness` 1: an ellipse, 0: a rectangle). From `radius` the pixel is mixed
toward `color`, rising over `feather` along a smoothstep to `amount`.

Parameters
----------
amount : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    How far the edges are mixed toward the colour where the vignette is fullest: 0 =
    off, 0.5 = halfway, 1 = the colour itself at the corners.
radius : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    Where the vignette starts, as a fraction of the distance from the centre to a
    corner: everything inside is untouched. Raising it clears more of the picture; 1
    leaves even the corners alone.
feather : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 0.5
    How far beyond the radius the vignette takes to reach full strength, as a fraction
    of the distance from the centre to a corner (a smooth S-curve). 0 = a hard edge;
    raising it softens the transition.
roundness : number, fraction, 0 to 1, default 1.0
    The shape of the vignette's contours: 1 = an ellipse that follows the picture's
    proportions, 0 = a rectangle, which darkens the middle of each edge as much as a
    corner; in between, a rounded rectangle.
color : colour, default '#000000'
    The colour the edges fade to: '#rrggbb', '#rgb' or [r, g, b] 0..1. Black darkens,
    white lightens."""
    ...

def white_balance(temperature: float = 0.0, tint: float = 0.0) -> Step:
    """Shift the colour balance by `temperature` (warm-cool) and `tint` (green-magenta), in stops of linear light.

This is von Kries scaling: the picture is decoded to linear light (sRGB, IEC 61966-2-1), each channel is multiplied
by a gain, and it is encoded again. The gains are red 2**(temperature/2), green 2**-tint and blue
2**(-temperature/2), divided by the largest of the three, so the result is never brighter than the input, never
clips, and a white pixel keeps one full channel instead of turning into a coloured cast. The defaults give gains
of 1: no change.

Parameters
----------
temperature : number, stops, -2 to 2, default 0.0
    Warm-cool shift in stops (-2..2): the difference in stops between the red and the
    blue gain, shared evenly (red up and blue down by half of it each). Positive warms
    the picture (more orange), negative cools it (more blue), 0 leaves the colour
    balance alone. Use a negative value to correct a picture shot under warm light.
tint : number, stops, -1 to 1, default 0.0
    Green-magenta shift in stops (-1..1): the green gain is multiplied by 2 to the power
    of minus tint. Positive adds magenta (less green), negative adds green, 0 leaves the
    balance alone."""
    ...
